</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The GoC five-year yield has risen 20 basis points over the past year, reaching 3.15% on June 9, even as the Bank of Canada has held the overnight rate unchanged since December 2025. Fixed mortgage renewal rates, which trail this benchmark by a lender spread of 120 to 180 basis points, have risen accordingly.</p>

<p>The GoC five-year bond yield has tracked above 3.00% since October 2025 and closed at 3.15% on June 9. That is 20 basis points higher than a year ago, a move that has pushed fixed renewal rates to levels most renewing clients have not encountered before. The hold at 2.25% does not mean the renewal environment is benign. It means the overnight rate is stable while the five-year yield, which is what drives fixed mortgage pricing, has already moved.</p>

<h2>Why Stability Is the Most Dangerous Environment</h2>

<p>Shefrin and Statman''s 1985 paper in the Journal of Finance on what they called the disposition effect documented the systematic tendency to hold losing positions too long and sell winning positions too early, a pattern driven by the same loss-aversion mechanism that produces status quo bias. The common thread is avoidance of regret. Selling a winner produces the regret of leaving money on the table. Holding a loser avoids the regret of crystallizing a loss. Staying with the existing mortgage lender avoids the regret of going through a comparison process and possibly not finding a better offer.</p>

<p>This is the architecture of the problem. The client who accepts the renewal offer from their existing lender without shopping alternatives has not made a rational calculation. They have made a default choice dressed up as a rational one. The advisor''s role is to make that visible. Not to prescribe what the client should do, but to make the cost of not asking the question explicit before the envelope gets opened.</p>

<p>The July 15 Bank of Canada decision is the next potential catalyst. If the May CPI print, due June 22, shows continued energy-driven elevation, rate-hike pricing will build modestly in financial markets, as it has already begun to with roughly 40 basis points of hikes priced into swaps by year end according to Bloomberg data. But that is a market expectation, not a certainty, and waiting for certainty is itself a form of status quo bias. The clients renewing in July through September do not have until July 15 to decide.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with mortgages coming up for renewal in the next 90 days are experiencing a quiet form of dread. The rate environment is not collapsing, which means the anxiety has no obvious target and tends to manifest as paralysis rather than urgency. They know the payment will be higher. They do not know how much higher. They have not called anyone yet because nothing has happened to make calling feel necessary. That flat, unresolved tension is what status quo bias feeds on.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to flag something before your renewal comes up. The Bank of Canada held again today, which is good news, but the rate that actually matters for your renewal is the five-year Government of Canada bond yield, not the overnight rate. That yield has been running above 3% since last fall and closed at 3.15% yesterday. What that means practically is that fixed mortgage rates for new terms are in the 4.4 to 4.9 range right now. Your existing lender will send you a renewal offer and it will probably land somewhere in that range. What most people don''t realize is that the first offer is rarely their best option. I''ve seen clients save between 20 and 50 basis points just by asking the question before they sign. That''s roughly $2,000 to $5,000 over the term on a $600,000 balance. I''m not pushing you toward any specific lender. I just don''t want you to sign the first offer because it arrived and doing nothing felt easier than doing something. Can we spend 20 minutes walking through the numbers before you receive that renewal?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with five-year fixed mortgages maturing in mid-2026 who locked in at pandemic-era rates of 1.8% to 2.2%. These clients face the largest payment shock regardless of which lender they renew with and have the most to gain from rate comparison.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients who have already had one renewal since 2022 and are now renewing a second time. Their previous renewal partially absorbed the rate shock; this renewal adds incrementally to a payment level they have already adjusted to, but the status quo bias still applies.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients whose income has risen substantially since 2021 may qualify for better rate tiers than they held originally, making the case for shopping particularly strong.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull a list of clients with mortgage renewal dates in the next 120 days and prioritize outreach before their lender makes first contact.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-page comparison tool showing the cost difference between accepting a first offer and a rate 25 basis points lower on the client''s current balance.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document all conversations about mortgage renewal strategy as part of the client file, noting the client''s stated preference and any referrals to mortgage specialists.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the June 22 Statistics Canada CPI release and July 15 Bank of Canada decision as the next two dates that could shift client urgency and adjust outreach timing accordingly.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your mortgage renewal and the number that actually matters<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada held its rate at 2.25% this morning as expected. I wanted to follow up on our conversation because the hold creates a quiet window that most people miss.<br><br>
The rate your lender will offer at renewal is driven by the five-year Government of Canada bond yield, which has been running above 3% for months and closed at 3.15% yesterday. Fixed renewal rates are currently in the 4.4 to 4.9 range. Your existing lender will send an offer. Historically, shopping that offer before accepting it results in savings of 20 to 50 basis points for borrowers who take the time to ask.<br><br>
On a $600,000 balance, 30 basis points over a five-year term is approximately $3,200 in interest. I wanted to make sure you had that number before the envelope arrived.<br><br>
I am happy to walk through the renewal math with you, or to connect you with a mortgage specialist who can run a full comparison. Just let me know if that would be useful.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Mortgage holders at first renewal since 2021:</strong> The largest and most time-sensitive group. Clients who have not yet experienced a renewal in the current rate environment and are about to. The payment shock will be real and the advisory window is now, not after they sign.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors with a mortgage:</strong> DIY investors often manage their portfolios actively while treating their mortgage as a fixed background cost. The behavioral contrast is instructive and opens a conversation about the asymmetry of attention most households allocate to financial decisions.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with variable-rate mortgages:</strong> This group has benefited from the BoC''s cuts from 5.00% to 2.25% and may be vulnerable to the assumption that rates will stay low. The swap market is now pricing 40 basis points of hikes by year end, which changes the variable-vs-fixed calculus.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada held again this morning. I''m calling because, counterintuitively, a hold is actually when the mortgage renewal mistake most often gets made, and I wanted to make sure you had the context before your renewal landed.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The research on status quo bias is unambiguous: when the environment is stable and no external event forces a decision, people default to inaction. In the mortgage context, inaction means accepting the first renewal offer. For a borrower at the scale of most advisory clients, the cost of that default choice is typically measured in thousands of dollars over the term.</p>
<p>An advisor who surfaces this before the renewal envelope arrives is delivering value that no rate platform, robo-advisor, or DIY investor can replicate. The behavioral intervention is the product. The rate comparison is the mechanism.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>When does your current mortgage term end?</p>
<p>Have you heard from your lender yet about renewal options?</p>
<p>When you renewed in [prior term], did you compare rates before signing or go with your existing lender''s offer?</p>
<p>How much has your income changed since you originally took out this mortgage?</p>
<p>Are you thinking about any major changes in the next five years, like moving, downsizing, or changes to the property?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Bank of Canada held today. Here is what that means for your mortgage renewal.<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada held its overnight rate at 2.25% this morning. For most borrowers, a hold feels like good news because nothing changed. The research on financial decision-making suggests the opposite is often true.<br><br>
When rates are stable, the strongest default tendency kicks in: accept whatever the current lender offers. For the hundreds of thousands of Canadians renewing five-year terms this year, the default choice typically comes at a cost of 20 to 50 basis points compared to the rate available to a borrower who shops before signing. On a $700,000 balance, that is between $3,500 and $8,750 over the term.<br><br>
I work with clients on exactly this kind of decision, not just portfolio management but the full picture of how wealth is structured. If your mortgage is renewing in the next six months, I''d be glad to walk through the numbers with you.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
