VALUES (
  '2026/06/10/the-renewal-math-advisors-arent-running',
  'tax', 'article',
  'The Renewal Math Advisors Aren''t Running',
  'With the Bank of Canada holding at 2.25% and the GoC five-year yield at 3.15%, the 2026 mortgage renewal wave has a tax dimension most planning conversations are missing.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The Bank of Canada holds at 2.25% this morning,</strong><span> the seventh consecutive hold, but the GoC five-year bond yield at 3.15% means fixed renewal rates are running 4.35% to 4.95%, a material increase for clients renewing pandemic-era terms.</span></li>
<li><strong>The tax dimension of mortgage renewal is systematically underweighted in planning conversations,</strong><span> specifically for clients with corporate accounts, rental properties, or investment portfolios that could be repositioned to optimize after-tax debt servicing costs.</span></li>
<li><strong>For clients who hold interest-bearing investments inside a non-registered account while carrying a non-deductible mortgage,</strong><span> the Smith Manoeuvre or a structured debt-swap approach may reduce after-tax carrying costs by converting non-deductible interest into deductible investment interest under the Income Tax Act.</span></li>
<li><strong>FHSA holders approaching their first home purchase face a 2026-specific planning window:</strong><span> contributions made before the purchase deadline are deductible, reducing taxable income in a year when energy-driven inflation has pushed CRA''s prescribed rate to 5%.</span></li>
<li><strong>The 2026 renewal wave is not only a cash-flow conversation,</strong><span> it is a tax and asset-location conversation, and the advisors who frame it that way will own the planning relationship that the mortgage broker cannot.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The Bank of Canada''s decision to hold its overnight rate at 2.25% removes the immediate catalyst that would push clients to call their advisors about mortgage strategy. That is the planning problem. The renewal wave arriving in 2026 is the largest in Canadian mortgage history by volume, and the clients renewing pandemic-era five-year fixed terms into a GoC five-year yield of 3.15% are facing a structurally different interest cost environment. The tax implications of how they finance, structure, and respond to that cost have not entered most planning conversations.</p>

<h2>The Deductibility Question</h2>

<p>Under section 20(1)(c) of the Income Tax Act, interest paid on money borrowed to earn income from a business or property is deductible. Interest paid on a mortgage secured against a principal residence is not. This distinction is foundational to the Smith Manoeuvre, a debt-conversion strategy developed by Fraser Smith and widely discussed in Canadian financial planning circles, which restructures a non-deductible mortgage into a deductible investment loan over time.</p>

<p>The mechanics are straightforward: as the client pays down their mortgage principal each period, they borrow that same amount back through a home equity line of credit and invest the proceeds in income-producing assets. The HELOC interest, because the borrowed funds are used to earn investment income, is deductible under section 20(1)(c). Over a five-year renewal term, a client with $400,000 remaining on a mortgage making scheduled principal payments of approximately $3,400 per month would convert roughly $204,000 of non-deductible debt to deductible debt. At a 4.5% HELOC rate and a 45% marginal tax rate, the annual after-tax interest saving on that converted balance reaches approximately $4,600 by the end of the term.</p>

<p>This is not a universally appropriate strategy. It requires the client to have suitable investment knowledge, a HELOC facility, discipline to maintain the reborrowment cycle, and an income tax profile where the deduction is meaningful. But for the right client profile, a renewal conversation that ignores this planning dimension is an incomplete conversation.</p>

<h2>The Rental Property Dimension</h2>

<p>Clients who hold rental properties face a different but related tax question at renewal. Interest on mortgages secured against income-producing rental properties is deductible against rental income under section 20(1)(c). The renewal decision for a rental property therefore has a direct after-tax cost calculation that differs materially from a principal residence renewal.</p>

<p>At a renewal rate of 4.7% on a $500,000 rental property mortgage, the annual gross interest cost is approximately $23,500. For a client in the 50% combined federal-provincial marginal bracket, the after-tax cost of that interest is approximately $11,750, assuming the rental income is sufficient to absorb the deduction. Locking in for a shorter term at a slightly higher rate versus a longer term at a lower rate carries different risk profiles when the deductibility is factored in.</p>

<p>The planning question worth raising at renewal for rental property clients is whether the current structure of their portfolio optimizes the deductible debt against assets generating the highest taxable income. A client who holds GICs in a non-registered account generating fully taxable interest while servicing a deductible rental mortgage is not in the worst position, but a repositioning that moves those GICs into a TFSA while maintaining the rental mortgage structure could improve after-tax returns without changing the gross investment exposure.</p>

<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">AFTER-TAX MORTGAGE COST: DEDUCTIBLE VS NON-DEDUCTIBLE</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">$500K balance</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#c0392b;">▼ 2.35pp after-tax gap at 47% MTR</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">GROSS VS NET &nbsp;|&nbsp; 4.70% RENEWAL RATE</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
<script>
(function(){
  // Horizontal bar: gross vs after-tax cost for deductible and non-deductible mortgage
  // at four marginal tax rates, $500K balance, 4.70% rate
  // gross interest: $23,500 / year
  var gross = 23500;
  var mtrs = [
    {label:"33% MTR", mtr:0.33},
    {label:"40% MTR", mtr:0.40},
    {label:"47% MTR", mtr:0.47},
    {label:"53% MTR", mtr:0.53}
  ];
  var n = mtrs.length;
  var margin = {left:80,right:24,top:22,bottom:46};
  var W = 680, H = 300;
  var PW = W - margin.left - margin.right;
  var PH = H - margin.top - margin.bottom;
  var barGroupH = PH / n;
  var barH = barGroupH * 0.32;
  var maxVal = 26000;

  var svg = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","svg");
  svg.setAttribute("viewBox","0 0 680 300");
  svg.setAttribute("width","100%");
  svg.setAttribute("preserveAspectRatio","xMidYMid meet");

  function el(tag, attrs){
    var e = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", tag);
    for(var k in attrs) e.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]);
    return e;
  }
  function txt(text, attrs){
    var e = el("text", attrs);
    e.textContent = text;
    return e;
  }
  function fmtK(v){ return "$" + Math.round(v/1000*10)/10 + "K"; }

  var xS = function(v){ return margin.left + (v/maxVal)*PW; };

  // Gridlines
  [5000,10000,15000,20000,25000].forEach(function(v){
    var x = xS(v);
    svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:x,x2:x,y1:margin.top,y2:margin.top+PH,stroke:"#ececec","stroke-width":"0.5"}));
    svg.appendChild(txt(fmtK(v),{x:x,y:margin.top+PH+12,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"8","fill":"#999","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  });

  // Axis
  svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left+PW,y1:margin.top+PH,y2:margin.top+PH,stroke:"#d8d8d8","stroke-width":"1"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left,y1:margin.top,y2:margin.top+PH,stroke:"#d8d8d8","stroke-width":"1"}));

  // X axis label
  svg.appendChild(txt("Annual interest cost ($)",{x:margin.left+PW/2,y:margin.top+PH+26,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"8","fill":"#999","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));

  mtrs.forEach(function(row, i){
    var afterTax = gross * (1 - row.mtr);
    var midY = margin.top + i*barGroupH + barGroupH/2;
    var grossY = midY - barH - 2;
    var netY = midY + 2;

    // Gross bar (non-deductible): slate
    svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:margin.left,y:grossY,width:xS(gross)-margin.left,height:barH,fill:"#4a5568"}));
    // Net bar (deductible): green
    svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:margin.left,y:netY,width:xS(afterTax)-margin.left,height:barH,fill:"#3a7a55"}));

    // Labels on bars
    svg.appendChild(txt(fmtK(gross),{x:xS(gross)+4,y:grossY+barH/2+3,"text-anchor":"start","font-size":"8","fill":"#444","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
    svg.appendChild(txt(fmtK(afterTax),{x:xS(afterTax)+4,y:netY+barH/2+3,"text-anchor":"start","font-size":"8","fill":"#3a7a55","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));

    // Row label
    svg.appendChild(txt(row.label,{x:margin.left-6,y:midY+3,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":"8.5","fill":"#444","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  });

  // Legend
  svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:margin.left+PW-180,y:margin.top+4,width:10,height:8,fill:"#4a5568"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt("Non-deductible (gross)",{x:margin.left+PW-167,y:margin.top+11,"font-size":"8","fill":"#444","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:margin.left+PW-180,y:margin.top+16,width:10,height:8,fill:"#3a7a55"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt("Deductible (after-tax)",{x:margin.left+PW-167,y:margin.top+23,"font-size":"8","fill":"#444","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));

  // Gold pill on 47% MTR after-tax bar (most relevant advisor client)
  var pill_i = 2; // 47% MTR
  var pillRow = mtrs[pill_i];
  var pillAfterTax = gross * (1 - pillRow.mtr);
  var pillMidY = margin.top + pill_i*barGroupH + barGroupH/2;
  var pillNetY = pillMidY + 2;
  var pillX = xS(pillAfterTax) - 64;
  var pillY = pillNetY + barH/2 - 8;
  svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:pillX,y:pillY,width:60,height:16,rx:"3",fill:"#e8a825"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt(fmtK(pillAfterTax),{x:pillX+30,y:pillY+11,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"9","font-weight":"700","fill":"#111","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));

  var container = document.currentScript ? document.currentScript.parentNode : null;
  if(container) container.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Income Tax Act s.20(1)(c); renewal rate assumption 4.70% on $500,000 balance, annual gross interest $23,500. After-tax cost computed at four combined federal-provincial marginal rates. hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">For a client at the 47% combined marginal tax rate with an income-producing property, a $23,500 annual gross interest cost carries an after-tax burden of $12,455, compared to $23,500 for a client in an identical position with a non-deductible principal residence mortgage. The gap widens at higher marginal rates.</p>

<h2>FHSA and the 2026 Prescribed Rate Window</h2>

<p>The First Home Savings Account introduced a planning wrinkle that has not fully integrated into renewal-era conversations. Clients who opened an FHSA in 2023 or 2024 and have not yet made a qualifying first home purchase have accumulated deductible contribution room. CRA''s prescribed rate, which influences various planning calculations including family loans, currently stands at 5% for Q2 and Q3 2026.</p>

<p>For clients navigating the FHSA in the current rate environment, the deductibility of contributions reduces taxable income in a year when energy-driven headline inflation has pushed CPI to 2.8% and the political pressure on indexed benefits is elevated. The planning question is whether a client who has maximized RRSP and TFSA room and is also managing a mortgage renewal in 2026 should accelerate FHSA contributions to maximize the deduction in the current tax year before rates move.</p>

<p>The maximum annual FHSA contribution is $8,000, with a lifetime maximum of $40,000. A client at a 47% combined marginal rate who contributes the maximum in calendar 2026 captures a deduction worth approximately $3,760 in the current tax year, which can be applied against the income driving their marginal rate. For clients whose renewal has increased monthly housing costs by $600 or more, the effective tax saving from a full FHSA contribution partially offsets the renewal payment increase in the same tax year.</p>

<h2>The Broader Asset Location Question</h2>

<p>Renewal season raises the asset location conversation in a specific form. Clients carrying a non-deductible mortgage at 4.5% to 4.9% while holding fully taxable interest income in a non-registered account are paying a real after-tax cost that a repositioning could reduce. The calculation is not complex: a client earning 4.5% on a GIC inside a non-registered account at a 47% marginal rate is netting approximately 2.39% after tax. If that same client is servicing a non-deductible mortgage at 4.5%, the net cost of the mortgage exceeds the net return on the GIC by the full 47% tax drag.</p>

<p>Moving the GIC into a TFSA or RRSP while maintaining the mortgage does not reduce the mortgage payment, but it eliminates the taxable interest income, improving the overall after-tax picture. This conversation sits squarely in the advisory value zone that a mortgage broker, a bank renewal officer, and a robo-advisor cannot occupy. The advisor who holds the full financial picture is the only party in a position to run this calculation for the client.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients facing a mortgage renewal in 2026 are focused on the headline payment increase and not on the tax structure underneath it. They are anxious about cash flow, not about planning. The advisor who surfaces the deductibility dimension shifts the client''s orientation from "how do I absorb this cost" to "how do I reduce it," which is a more productive posture and one that reinforces the advisor''s value as more than a rate-watcher.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Before we talk about what rate you''ll renew at, I want to walk through a question that most clients don''t get asked: is the interest on this mortgage deductible? For your principal residence, it''s not. But there are a few ways to look at the total picture that might change how we think about structuring your assets around the renewal. If you hold any interest-bearing investments outside a registered account while you''re carrying this mortgage, the after-tax math may not be working in your favour. I also want to ask about the rental property, because that interest is deductible and the renewal decision there has a different calculation than the family home. Can we spend 30 minutes going through both properties together and looking at where the deductible and non-deductible debt sit relative to your asset mix? I''d rather do that now than after you''ve signed both renewals.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with both a principal residence mortgage and one or more rental property mortgages renewing in 2026. The deductibility gap between the two creates a planning priority that must be addressed before the renewal documents are signed.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with FHSA accounts who have not yet made a qualifying purchase. They have a deductibility window in the current tax year that closes at purchase; maximizing contributions before renewal adds a partial offset to the higher payment.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with material non-registered interest income and a non-deductible mortgage. A repositioning of interest-generating assets into registered accounts reduces taxable income while maintaining the same gross investment exposure.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For each client with a mortgage renewing in 2026, flag whether the property is a principal residence or income-producing, as the deductibility status determines the planning approach.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull a list of clients holding non-registered GICs or fixed-income products and cross-reference against clients with non-deductible mortgages to identify asset-location inefficiencies worth addressing before year-end.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check FHSA contribution room for eligible clients and confirm whether the 2026 contribution deadline has been met; the tax year-end deduction is worth $3,760 at a 47% marginal rate for the full $8,000 contribution.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients with rental property mortgages, document the deductibility analysis as part of the planning file and note whether a shorter or longer renewal term changes the net after-tax cost calculation.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your mortgage renewal: the tax layer most clients miss<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
With the Bank of Canada holding at 2.25% this morning, I wanted to follow up with something that rarely comes up in renewal conversations but can change the numbers materially.<br><br>
Whether or not the interest on your mortgage is tax-deductible determines your real after-tax cost of carrying that debt. For a principal residence, it is not deductible. For a rental property, it is, under s.20(1)(c) of the Income Tax Act. At your marginal tax rate, a $23,500 annual interest cost on a rental property mortgage translates to an after-tax cost closer to $12,400.<br><br>
Separately, if you hold interest-bearing investments in a non-registered account while carrying a non-deductible mortgage, the net after-tax math on those two positions combined may not be working as efficiently as it could. That''s a 30-minute conversation worth having before you sign any renewal documents.<br><br>
If you have an FHSA with unused contribution room, the 2026 tax year deduction for a full $8,000 contribution is approximately $3,760 at your rate. That deduction can offset some of the higher renewal payment in the same tax year.<br><br>
Happy to walk through all of this with you. Let me know when you have time.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice. Tax implications depend on individual circumstances; please consult a qualified tax professional before acting on any information provided.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Rental property investors renewing in 2026:</strong> Clients who own one or more income-producing properties and have not been walked through the deductibility advantage relative to their principal residence. This group has a tax calculation sitting in plain sight that most renewal conversations never surface.</p>
<p><strong>High-income professionals with non-registered fixed income:</strong> Clients holding GICs, bond ETFs, or savings accounts outside registered structures while carrying a non-deductible mortgage are in an asset-location inefficiency that an advisor can resolve in one conversation.</p>
<p><strong>FHSA holders approaching first purchase:</strong> Clients within two to three years of a first home purchase who have open FHSA accounts and have not maximized the 2026 contribution. The deduction is real, the deadline is the tax year, and the conversation is time-sensitive.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m calling because the Bank of Canada held again today and most people are going to treat that as a signal to do nothing with their mortgage. I wanted to reach out before you get your renewal offer because the tax structure around your properties is something a renewal officer won''t touch, and I think it''s worth 20 minutes before you sign.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A mortgage renewal officer''s job is to close the renewal at the best rate available from their institution. A bank''s renewal team does not have visibility into the client''s full tax picture, registered account balances, or asset location. The deductibility analysis, the FHSA calculation, and the asset repositioning conversation are all exclusively in the advisor''s lane.</p>
<p>For a client at the 47% marginal rate with $500,000 in rental property debt, the difference between structuring the renewal correctly and not is approximately $11,000 in after-tax interest cost annually. That is the dollar value of the conversation the advisor is uniquely positioned to have.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you own any properties beyond your primary residence?</p>
<p>Are you renewing any mortgages in the next six months?</p>
<p>Where do you hold your fixed-income or interest-bearing investments right now?</p>
<p>Have you opened an FHSA, and if so, are you expecting to use it in the next two or three years?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through the tax deductibility of your mortgage interest recently?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The tax question no one asks at mortgage renewal<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada held its rate at 2.25% this morning. If you have a mortgage renewing in 2026, the hold probably feels neutral. Most renewal conversations stop there.<br><br>
There is a tax question worth asking before you sign: is your mortgage interest deductible? For a principal residence, it is not. For a rental property, it is under the Income Tax Act, and the after-tax cost of identical gross interest payments can differ by almost half depending on your marginal rate and the property type.<br><br>
If you also hold interest-bearing investments in a non-registered account while carrying a non-deductible mortgage, the combined after-tax picture is likely inefficient in a way that a 20-minute conversation could address.<br><br>
I work with clients on the full structure of their financial picture, not just portfolio returns. If any of this is relevant to your situation, I''d be glad to talk through it.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice. Tax implications depend on individual circumstances; please consult a qualified tax professional before acting on any information provided.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
