<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Headline CPI jumped to 2.8% in April 2026, driven by a 28.6% year-over-year increase in gasoline prices following the Hormuz closure in late February. Core inflation, the average of CPI-median and CPI-trim, cooled to 2.1% in the same month, reinforcing the BoC''s read that energy is doing the work and broader price pressures remain contained.</p>

<h2>The Jobs Number That Changed the Calculus</h2>

<p>The May Labour Force Survey, released June 5, was the most consequential data point ahead of today''s decision. Canada added 88,000 net jobs in May against a consensus forecast of 10,000. The unemployment rate fell to 6.6% from 6.9%. The magnitude of the miss, nearly nine times the consensus, was large enough to close the rate-cut discussion entirely for the June meeting and introduce a credible, if still unlikely, probability of a hike in July or later in the year.</p>

<p>RBC Economics responded with a note from assistant chief economist Nathan Janzen and economist Abbey Xu saying the Bank would "remain cautious" and keep rates on hold for the rest of 2026, but acknowledged a hike was possible if trade and energy risks resolved in a way that allowed the economy to run hotter. True North Mortgage''s rate forecast tracker, updated June 8, noted that average wage growth in May cooled sharply to 3.0% year-over-year from 4.5% in April, suggesting the jobs added were concentrated in lower-wage categories, which tempers the inflation risk from the headline employment figure.</p>

<p>The Bank of Canada''s own April 29 Monetary Policy Report projected CPI averaging 2.3% in 2026, peaking around 3.0% in April before declining to 2.5% in June and returning to 2.0% by early 2027. That forecast was built on a Brent crude assumption of approximately $90 in Q2. Brent has traded between $92 and $98 through much of the period since that projection, and the June 9 close near $93 is broadly consistent with the baseline. The May CPI print due June 22 will be the first test of whether the BoC''s trajectory is holding.</p>

<h2>The Transmission to Fixed Mortgage Rates</h2>

<p>The overnight rate hold at 2.25% is not the number that matters most for the 30% of Canadian mortgage holders renewing in 2026. The five-year Government of Canada bond yield matters more, and it has moved independently of the policy rate. The GoC five-year yield closed at 3.15% on June 9, 20 basis points above its level a year ago, and has held above 3.00% since October 2025. The spread between that benchmark and current five-year fixed renewal rates is approximately 120 to 180 basis points, placing renewal rates in the 4.35% to 4.95% range.</p>

<p>Interest rate swap markets are now pricing approximately 40 basis points of hikes by year-end, according to Bloomberg data, a shift that has accumulated during the hold period and reflects market participants pricing the tail risk of an energy-driven inflation resurgence rather than a base case of tightening. If May CPI comes in above 3.0%, that pricing will move higher. The Governing Council''s next Monetary Policy Report, due July 15 alongside the rate decision, will update the baseline forecast with May data in hand.</p>

<p>The July 15 decision is the next scheduled pivot point. Between now and then, the June 22 CPI print, the June 30 monthly GDP reading for April, and any material development in the Hormuz situation will each have the capacity to shift the balance of risks the Bank is navigating. Today''s hold is not a signal of confidence that the path is clear. It is a signal that Governing Council does not yet have enough information to move in either direction without compounding a policy error it cannot easily reverse.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients are not following the nuance of the BoC''s hold. They hear "rates unchanged" and conclude nothing has changed. The clients most at risk are those with variable-rate mortgages or HELOCs who are relieved by the hold without understanding that swap markets are now pricing hikes by Q4. Clients with fixed-rate renewals coming up are focused on the payment increase rather than on the rate path risk that will determine whether they should lock in now or wait.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada held this morning, which is what everyone expected. I want to make sure you have the full picture, because "nothing changed" is a bit misleading. The overnight rate is unchanged at 2.25%, but bond markets have been pricing in roughly 40 basis points of hikes by year-end, and the five-year bond yield that drives your fixed mortgage rate has already risen 20 basis points over the past year. The next big data point is CPI on June 22. If energy prices kept pushing inflation above 3% in May, the hold-or-hike conversation at the July 15 meeting becomes real in a way it hasn''t been since 2023. I''m not telling you a hike is coming. The base case is still a hold through year-end. But if you have a variable rate or a renewal coming up and you''ve been waiting for rates to fall further, the data that would support that view has not materialized.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with variable-rate mortgages or HELOCs who took comfort from the BoC''s easing cycle and have not revisited their rate risk since the hold period began. The 40 basis points of hikes now priced in swaps represents a non-trivial shift in their exposure.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients renewing fixed-rate terms in H2 2026. The hold is neutral for them in the immediate term, but if swap pricing continues to build and the July 15 decision introduces a hawkish tilt in the MPR language, the renewal window they are in may close on better terms than what follows.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who locked in fixed rates in late 2025 or early 2026 when the GoC five-year yield was near 2.87% to 2.95%. Their positioning looks increasingly defensible relative to current renewal rates.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note June 22 as a critical date for clients with variable-rate exposure; set a calendar reminder to contact those clients the morning of the CPI release with context before they see the headline.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients renewing fixed terms in July through September 2026, prepare a brief showing current available rates against the swap-market implied path; the case for not waiting may be stronger than the client currently appreciates.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review all client files with variable-rate debt and document the current rate, the trigger points that would produce a payment change, and the client''s stated preference regarding rate risk.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note July 15 as the next scheduled BoC decision and flag it as a potential portfolio discussion trigger for clients in income-sensitive positions.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Bank of Canada holds at 2.25%: what the decision means and what to watch next<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada held its overnight rate at 2.25% this morning, the seventh consecutive hold since December 2025. The decision was widely anticipated and reflects the same policy tension that has defined the year: Canada''s economy is in a technical recession by one definition while running CPI at 2.8% and adding 88,000 jobs in May.<br><br>
A few things worth noting beyond the headline.<br><br>
The rate that matters for most fixed mortgage renewals is the five-year Government of Canada bond yield, not the overnight rate. That yield is at 3.15% today, 20 basis points higher than a year ago. Fixed renewal rates are running between 4.35% and 4.95%.<br><br>
Bond markets are now pricing roughly 40 basis points of hikes by year-end. That is not the base case, but it is a shift worth tracking.<br><br>
The next key date is June 22, when Statistics Canada releases May CPI. If energy prices held elevated in May, the Bank''s July 15 decision becomes more complex. I will be in touch after that release with any implications for your situation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Variable-rate mortgage holders who went DIY on rate strategy:</strong> Clients who chose variable rates in 2024-2025 based on the BoC easing cycle and have no advisor overseeing their rate risk. The hold environment plus the emerging hike pricing is exactly the kind of nuanced signal a DIY investor tends to miss.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners managing corporate cash:</strong> The rate hold affects the cost of business credit lines, the return on corporate cash holdings, and the timing of capital investment decisions. A business owner without an advisor is navigating this without a framework.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees in decumulation with interest-rate-sensitive income:</strong> GIC and fixed-income yields have been shaped by the BoC hold and the GoC five-year yield level. The emerging hike pricing is relevant to how they ladder maturities and whether to lock in current rates before they move.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada held this morning, which most people will read as nothing happened. I''m calling because the story underneath the hold is more interesting than the headline, and it has a direct implication for anyone managing rate-sensitive assets or debt right now.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A Bank of Canada decision day is one of the eight moments each year when the gap between a client with an advisor and a client without one is most visible. The headline is available to everyone. The transmission mechanism, the swap-market pricing, the GoC five-year yield divergence, the May CPI trigger date, and the July 15 framework are not. An advisor who calls the day of the decision with that context is demonstrating a service that no rate comparison site or robo-advisor can replicate.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you have variable-rate debt right now, including a mortgage or credit line?</p>
<p>When you heard the Bank held this morning, what was your read on what that means for your situation?</p>
<p>Do you have GICs or fixed-income holdings maturing in the next 12 months that will need to be reinvested?</p>
<p>Is there anyone helping you think through the rate environment in the context of your full financial picture?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Bank of Canada held today. Here is what the decision actually means.<br><br>
