<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI crude climbed from approximately $62 in early January to a peak near $109 in early April as the Hormuz closure took hold, then retreated to $89 by June 9 as ceasefire extensions and China''s reduced imports softened the demand signal. The $85 oil sands breakeven line marks the level below which integrated producer economics deteriorate materially.</p>

<h2>The Canadian Portfolio Implication</h2>

<p>WTI has traded above the approximate $85 oil sands integrated breakeven for the entirety of the Hormuz closure period. At current prices near $89, Canadian Natural Resources, Suncor Energy, and Cenovus Energy are generating free cash flow at rates that have produced extraordinary shareholder returns in 2026. Suncor reported Q1 2026 free funds flow up 53% year-over-year and net income of C$2.1 billion. The TSX energy sub-index gained approximately 27.4% on a year-to-date basis through end of May, making it among the top-performing energy sectors globally in the same period, according to the BBN Times analysis of June 9.</p>

<p>The CAD/USD exchange rate has not strengthened as dramatically as historical oil-CAD correlations would suggest. The pair was trading near 1.3940 on June 5-6, implying approximately $0.718 US per Canadian dollar, weaker than the correlation-implied level for WTI near $90. The divergence reflects the competing drag of Canada''s technical recession, the tariff uncertainty with the US, and the immigration-driven population decline that has reduced Canada''s structural growth rate in the current year. For Canadian investors holding energy equities, the combination of high oil prices and a relatively weak CAD amplifies the USD-denominated commodity revenue relative to CAD-denominated costs, which is positive for free cash flow but does not translate proportionally into CAD portfolio returns for domestic investors.</p>

<h2>Calibrating the Tail Risk: Resolution</h2>

<p>The base case for Canadian energy investors is continuation of the current environment through at least the summer: Hormuz closed or minimally functional, WTI in the $85 to $95 range, energy sector free cash flow elevated, and BoC inflation pressures keeping rates from falling. That base case is priced into current energy equity valuations.</p>

<p>The tail risk worth calibrating is not further escalation. It is resolution. A genuine diplomatic breakthrough, whether a comprehensive US-Iran framework agreement or a unilateral US decision to lift the counter-blockade as negotiating leverage, could reprice WTI toward $65 to $70 rapidly. The Polymarket prediction markets as of June 9 were pricing WTI above $92.99 at less than 50% probability for the remainder of June, which implies the market is already partially pricing in some softening. A resolution scenario would hit that probability hard and fast.</p>

<p>For portfolios with material energy sector concentration that have been built up during the Hormuz premium period, the question is not whether energy has performed well. It has. The question is whether the position sizing reflects the optionality embedded in that premium or has come to be treated as a structural holding in a permanent high-oil-price regime. Those are different portfolio constructions with different risk profiles, and they require different conversations.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with energy exposure are pleased and inclined to hold or add. Clients without energy exposure are watching the TSX energy sector outperform and wondering whether they missed the trade. Both groups are underweighting the resolution tail risk. The advisor''s role is not to alarm either group but to ensure the position sizing reflects the scenario distribution, not just the base case.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The weekend escalation between Iran and Israel settled back down by Monday, and WTI is trading around $89 this morning. The Strait of Hormuz is still closed, the dual blockade is still in place, and Trump said this morning that Iran is taking too long to negotiate. So the base case for energy prices in the near term hasn''t changed materially. What I want to make sure we''re thinking about is the tail risk in the other direction. A genuine diplomatic resolution that removes the war premium would reprice oil toward $65 to $70 quickly, and the TSX energy names that have driven most of the composite''s performance this year would reprice with it. I''m not saying that''s the likely outcome. I''m saying it''s a scenario worth making sure your portfolio can absorb, because the energy position that looks brilliant in a closed-Hormuz world looks very different in an open-Hormuz world. Can we look at what percentage of your equity exposure is currently in the energy sector and whether that''s where we want it to be?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated TSX energy positions built up during the 2026 run. The 27% YTD gain in the energy sub-index through May has materially shifted portfolio weights for clients who have not rebalanced, and many will now be overweight energy relative to their stated risk profile.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients in balanced mandates with benchmark TSX exposure. The energy sector''s outperformance means even passive TSX exposure has become more energy-heavy; the drift may warrant a rebalancing conversation.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who were underweight energy through 2025 and have managed to stay disciplined. If resolution occurs, their relative positioning improves materially.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull current energy sector weighting for all client portfolios and flag any where energy now exceeds the stated target allocation by more than 5 percentage points due to price appreciation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients asking about adding energy exposure at current prices, prepare a scenario analysis showing the portfolio impact under the resolution tail risk scenario versus continued Hormuz closure.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note any client conversations where the energy sector is now being framed as a structural holding rather than a cyclical position; this framing shift is a behavioural risk worth documenting.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Watch Trump''s Iran statements and the Hormuz diplomatic calendar as the primary leading indicators for when the resolution risk becomes acute enough to warrant proactive rebalancing.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Iran-Israel weekend strikes: what settled down and what did not<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Iran and Israel exchanged strikes over the weekend, driving oil briefly above $98 before a mutual halt was re-established on Monday. WTI is trading near $89 this morning. The Strait of Hormuz remained closed throughout and is still closed today.<br><br>
The base case for energy prices has not changed materially: the dual blockade structure requires a substantive diplomatic deal to resolve, and Trump''s statement this morning that Iran is taking too long suggests pressure will continue rather than ease in the near term.<br><br>
What I wanted to flag is the tail risk in the other direction. A diplomatic breakthrough that removes the war premium would reprice WTI toward $65 to $70 quickly. Given the TSX energy sector''s 27% gain year-to-date through May, portfolios with meaningful energy exposure have seen significant drift toward that sector. It is worth confirming that the current weight reflects your intended risk profile rather than passive accumulation from price appreciation.<br><br>
I will follow up after the Bank of Canada decision at 9:45 this morning with any implications for your overall portfolio positioning.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
