The risk that is not in the energy sector headlines is what happens if the Strait of Hormuz reopens. A genuine diplomatic resolution between the US and Iran would remove the war premium embedded in WTI crude, repricing it from the current $89 toward $65 to $70 in short order. The same Canadian energy names that drove the TSX higher would reprice in the opposite direction with similar speed.<br><br>
The base case is continued closure through at least the summer. But a 30% downside scenario in the sector that now drives the TSX is worth having a framework for before it arrives.<br><br>
I work with clients on exactly this kind of positioning audit, making sure the current weight of any sector in a portfolio reflects an intentional risk decision rather than passive drift from outperformance. If that sounds useful, I''d be glad to connect.<br><br>
