  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>TSX-concentrated investors watching the composite pull back:</strong> Clients or prospects who have been heavily invested in Canadian equities through the 2026 energy-driven run and are now seeing the composite consolidate. The two-markets-in-one story explains why their portfolio may be behaving differently from the headline index move.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors who bought energy ETFs near the May high:</strong> Self-directed investors who added TSX or energy exposure at or near the 35,291 peak are sitting on modest losses and have no framework for whether this is a consolidation or the beginning of a more significant reversal. The distinction between an energy-driven pullback and a rate-driven pullback is one the advisor can make.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees with Canadian equity income exposure:</strong> Clients in drawdown who hold Canadian dividend-paying equities, particularly bank stocks and utilities, are experiencing both price pressure and dividend sustainability questions in a higher-for-longer rate environment that may extend.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The TSX is down about 2.5% from its May high and the Bank of Canada decision is this morning. I''m calling because the story inside the composite is more important than the headline number, and I wanted to make sure you had the context before markets opened.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A TSX headline that shows a 67-point decline tells an investor almost nothing useful. The advisor who can explain that the decline is concentrated in rate-sensitive financials and utilities rather than in the energy names, and who can frame the BoC decision language as the operative catalyst rather than the rate decision itself, is performing analysis that a brokerage platform or index fund dashboard cannot. The client who understands the two-markets-inside-the-composite story makes better decisions about whether to hold, rebalance, or add than the client who reads the headline number alone.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How much of your Canadian equity exposure is in bank stocks versus the energy sector right now?</p>
<p>When the TSX pulled back 2.3% on Friday, did you know which part of your portfolio drove that move?</p>
<p>Is anyone helping you understand the difference between a rate-driven market decline and an energy-driven one in the context of your holdings?</p>
<p>How are you thinking about the bond market''s pricing of potential hikes later this year relative to the Bank of Canada''s current hold?</p>
